<h1>Enya (1987)</h1>

<a class="new-amazon-album-link" href="https://amzn.to/3UBLYaD" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">
	<Image  src={ea} alt="The Celts (1992)"  width="300" height="300" /></a>

<p><strong><em>Enya</em></strong> is the self-titled debut studio album by Irish singer and composer <strong>Enya</strong>, released in <strong>March 1987</strong> by <strong>BBC Records</strong> in the UK and <strong>Atlantic Records</strong> in the US. It features music written and recorded for the soundtrack of the 1986 BBC documentary television series <a href="/the-celts-documentary/"><em>The Celts</em></a>.</p>

<p>Following her massive global commercial success, the record was remastered and reissued in <strong>November 1992</strong> under the title <strong><em><a href="/the-celts/">The Celts</a></em></strong> by <strong>Warner Music</strong>.</p>

<p><a href="https://amzn.to/3UBLYaD" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Stream or download Enya songs</a></p>

<h2>Commissioning and Background</h2>

<p>In 1985, producer <strong>Tony McAuley</strong> invited Enya to submit a single song for an upcoming BBC2 documentary series about Celtic history and culture.</p>

<p>Her submission, <strong>"March of the Celts"</strong>, impressed series director <strong>David Richardson</strong>, who commissioned her to compose the soundtrack for the entire six-part production.</p>

<h2>Recording Sessions and Production</h2>

<p>Enya recorded <strong>72 minutes</strong> of music over two years at <strong>Aigle Studio</strong> in Dublin and <strong>BBC Enterprises Studio Woodlands</strong> in London alongside her key partners, producer <strong>Nicky Ryan</strong> and lyricist <strong>Roma Ryan</strong>.</p>

<p>During sessions at the BBC studio, Nicky Ryan instructed sound engineers to disregard standard acoustic practices, employing extreme reverb settings of up to 24 seconds to build Enya's distinct multi-layered vocal soundscapes.</p>

<h2>Album Release Formats and Packaging</h2>

<p>The original album was released in <strong>March 1987</strong> to coincide with the television broadcast of the series, published on cassette, vinyl, and CD formats.</p>

<p>Two visual variations of the physical packaging were produced for the debut issue:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>BBC Records Edition</strong>: The original UK release featured a sleeve designed with a prominent <strong>white border</strong> around a photo of Enya.</li>
<li><strong>Atlantic Records Edition</strong>: The North American release was issued with a <strong>black-bordered cover</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Content Parity</strong>: The musical contents of both physical versions are completely identical.</li>
</ul>

<h2>1992 Reissue: <em>The Celts</em></h2>

<p>When <strong>Warner Music</strong> reissued the remastered album under the title <strong><em><a href="/the-celts/">The Celts</a></em></strong> in 1992, the record achieved new commercial success, peaking at number 10 on the <strong>UK Albums Chart</strong>.</p>

<p>On this edition, the original 1:23 track <strong>"Portrait"</strong> was replaced with a longer 3:12 track titled <strong>"Portrait (Out of the Blue)"</strong> featuring altered instrumentation.</p>

<p>As a result of this modification, the original 1987 pressing of <strong><em>Enya</em></strong> is no longer manufactured.</p>

<h2>Singles and Unreleased Tracks</h2>

<p>Because the debut album includes only 39 minutes of music from the original sessions, several tracks were issued separately over time:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>"I Want Tomorrow"</strong>: Released in March 1987 as Enya's debut solo single on 7-inch vinyl and CD formats.</li>
<li><strong>"The Celts"</strong>: Issued in November 1992 to promote the remastered reissue, peaking at number 29 on the UK Singles Chart.</li>
<li><strong>"Eclipse"</strong>: Featured as a B-side in 1992, created by playing the track "Deireadh An Tuath" in reverse.</li>
<li><strong>"Spaghetti Western Theme"</strong>: An unreleased piece in the style of Hugo Montenegro, included on the 2005 "Amarantine" CD single in memory of Tony McAuley.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Sampling Legacy of "Boadicea"</h2>

<p>The instrumental track <strong>"Boadicea"</strong> became one of the most sampled melodies in contemporary music history:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>The Fugees (1996)</strong>: Sampled the melody for <strong>"Ready or Not"</strong> on <em>The Score</em>. Enya planned legal action for unauthorized use but settled out of court, adding credit stickers to album copies.</li>
<li><strong>Mario Winans (2004)</strong>: Sampled the song for <strong>"I Don't Wanna Know"</strong>. Producer <strong>P. Diddy</strong> awarded Enya 60% royalties and co-featured credit, taking the song to <strong>#2</strong> on the Billboard Hot R&amp;B/Hip-Hop chart.</li>
<li><strong>The Pirates (2004)</strong>: Sampled the track for the answer song <strong>"You Should Really Know"</strong>, peaking at <strong>#8</strong> on the UK Singles Chart.</li>
<li><strong>Metro Boomin, The Weeknd &amp; 21 Savage (2022)</strong>: Heavily featured the sample on their chart-topping collaboration <strong>"Creepin'"</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Film and Media Soundtracks</h2>

<p>Music from <em>The Celts</em> soundtrack was later adapted for several independent film and media productions.</p>

<p>Instrumental compositions were reused for the soundtrack of the video production <em>The Memory of Earth</em>, produced by <strong>David Bickley</strong>.</p>

<p>In addition, the song <strong>"Boadicea"</strong> was featured on the soundtrack for the 1992 film <em>Sleepwalkers</em>, while <strong>"Epona"</strong> appeared in the 1991 movie <em>L.A. Story</em>.</p>

<p><strong>Single</strong>: <a href="#I_Want_Tomorrow">"I Want Tomorrow"</a> (1987)</p>

<p><strong><a href="/the-celts/#lyrics">Lyrics</a>, <a href="/the-celts/#videos">videos list</a> and <a href="/the-celts/#linernotes">liner notes</a> in <em><a href="/the-celts/">The Celts</a></em> album page</strong>.</p>

